  <p>
    Psoriasis is an immune-driven condition in which skin cells are produced much faster than they
    are shed. The result is thickened, red or dusky plaques with silvery scale, most often on the
    elbows, knees, lower back and scalp, though it can appear anywhere including the nails.
  </p>

  <p>
    It is not an infection, it is not caused by poor hygiene, and it cannot be passed to anyone
    else. Those three misconceptions cause more distress than the condition itself in many
    patients.
  </p>

  <h2>Forms it takes</h2>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Chronic plaque psoriasis</strong> — the common form. Well-defined scaly plaques on extensor surfaces.</li>
    <li><strong>Scalp psoriasis</strong> — thick scale along the hairline, often mistaken for severe dandruff.</li>
    <li><strong>Guttate psoriasis</strong> — a sudden shower of small spots, often a week or two after a throat infection. Frequently settles on its own.</li>
    <li><strong>Inverse psoriasis</strong> — in the folds, where it is shiny and red rather than scaly, and often misdiagnosed as fungal.</li>
    <li><strong>Nail psoriasis</strong> — pitting, discolouration and lifting. Commonly treated for months as a nail fungus before the diagnosis is made.</li>
    <li><strong>Pustular and erythrodermic psoriasis</strong> — uncommon, severe, and needing urgent in-person care.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>How it is assessed</h2>

  <p>
    Mostly clinically — the appearance, distribution and the characteristic scale are usually
    enough. Dermoscopy helps where it is unclear. A KOH mount is worth doing where fungus is in the
    differential, especially in the folds. A biopsy is occasionally needed when the picture is
    atypical or the response is unexpected.
  </p>

  <p>
    Because psoriasis is associated with joint disease, cardiovascular risk, diabetes and fatty
    liver, the consultation covers more than skin: joint symptoms, weight, blood pressure and
    relevant blood tests where indicated.
  </p>

  <h2>Treatment by severity</h2>

  <h3>Limited disease</h3>
  <p>
    Topical treatment: vitamin D analogues such as calcipotriol, topical steroids used in courses,
    coal tar preparations, and salicylic acid to lift thick scale so the active treatment can
    reach the skin. Emollients used generously make everything else work better.
  </p>

  <h3>Moderate disease</h3>
  <p>
    Phototherapy — narrowband UVB, given in a controlled course — is effective and well
    established. Oral methotrexate, acitretin or ciclosporin are used where phototherapy is not
    practical, each with its own monitoring requirements.
  </p>

  <h3>Severe or treatment-resistant disease</h3>
  <p>
    Biologics targeting specific parts of the immune pathway have changed outcomes substantially
    for severe psoriasis. They require screening before starting, including for tuberculosis, and
    ongoing monitoring.
  </p>

  <div class="note note-warn">
    <h3>One thing to avoid</h3>
    <p>
      Oral steroids are often prescribed for psoriasis because they clear it quickly. When they are
      stopped, the condition frequently rebounds worse than before, occasionally into a severe
      form. If a tablet cleared your psoriasis in days, bring the prescription to your
      consultation.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Living with it</h2>

  <p>
    Moisturising daily, treating throat infections promptly, stopping smoking and reducing alcohol
    all measurably reduce flares. So does having a plan for what to do when a flare starts, rather
    than restarting the search for a doctor each time.
  </p>
